# TypeScript & React Practices

Apply when editing `**/*.ts` or `**/*.tsx`.

## TypeScript

- Prefer **explicit types** for component props, hook returns, and public function signatures. Avoid `any` unless required by a third-party.
- **Error handling:** Do not use empty `catch`. Log and/or rethrow, or handle meaningfully.
- Use `const` by default; `let` only when reassignment is needed.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — typed props
interface CardProps { title: string; children?: React.ReactNode; }
export function Card({ title, children }: CardProps) { ... }

// ❌ BAD — empty catch
try { await fetch(); } catch (e) {}
```

## React

- **Functional components** only. Use hooks for state and side effects.
- **Exports:** Default for pages (`src/pages/*.tsx`); named for components, hooks, and utilities.
- Extract **custom hooks** for reusable stateful logic (e.g. `useToast`, `useIsMobile`). Keep components focused on rendering.
- Prefer `React.ReactNode` or explicit union types for `children` over untyped props.

## Files & organization

- One main component or hook per file. Colocate small helpers in the same file only if used solely there.
- For `index.ts` barrels: re-export only; no new logic. Keep `src/pages/index.ts` exports in sync with new pages.

## Async & data

- Handle loading and error states when fetching. Avoid leaving `undefined` or unhandled rejections in user-facing flows.
